orm to create a GitHub issue with structured data describing the correction. You will need a GitHub account. Once you create that issue, the correction will be reviewed by a staff member.\nL38: \nL39: ⚠️ Mobile Users: Submitting this form to create a new issue will only work with github.com, not the GitHub Mobile app.\nL40: Important: The Anthology treat PDFs as authoritative. Please use this form only to correct data that is out of line with the PDF. See cite20†our corrections guidelines if you need to change the PDF.\nL41: \nL42: Title Adjust the title. Retain tags such as <fixed-case>. [Input]\nL43: \nL44: Authors Adjust author names and order to match the PDF.\nL45: \nL46: [Button: Add Author]\nL47: Abstract Correct abstract if needed. Retain XML formatting tags such as <tex-math>. You may use <b>...</b> for bold, <i>...</i> for italic, <u>...</u> for underline, <sc>...</sc> for small-caps, <tt>...<tt> for `typewriter text`, <url>...</url> for URLs, <a href=...> for hyperlinks, and <par/> for paragraph breaks.\nL48: \nL49: Verification against PDF Ensure that the new title/authors match the snapshot below. (If there is no snapshot or it is too small, consult cite0†the PDF .)\nL50: Authors concatenated from the text boxes above:\nL51: \nL52: [Input] ALL author names match the snapshot above—including middle initials, hyphens, and accents.\nL53: \nL54: [Button: Create GitHub issue for staff review]\nL55: \nL56: * * *\nL57: ##### Abstract\nL58: We explore and enhance the ability of neural language models to generate novel scientific directions grounded in literature. Work on literature-based hypothesis generation has traditionally focused on binary link prediction—severely limiting the expressivity of hypotheses. This line of work also does not focus on optimizing novelty.\nL59: We take a dramatic departure with a novel setting in which models use as input background contexts (e.g., problems, experimental settings, goals), and output natural language ideas grounded in literature. We present SciMON, a modeling framework that uses retrieval of “inspirations” from past scientific papers, and explicitly optimizes for novelty by iteratively comparing to prior papers and updating idea suggestions until sufficient novelty is achieved.\nL60: Comprehensive evaluations reveal that GPT-4 tends to generate ideas with overall low technical depth and novelty, while our methods partially mitigate this issue. Our work represents a first step toward evaluating and developing language models that generate new ideas derived from the scientific literature. Code, data, and resources are publicly available for research purposes: cite28†https://github.com/eaglew/clbd†github.com .\nL61: Anthology ID:\nL62:     2024.acl-long.18\nL63: Volume:\nL64:     cite29†Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers) L65: Month:\nL66:     August\nL67: Year:\nL68:     2024\nL69: Address:\nL70:     Bangkok, Thailand\nL71: Editors:\nL72:     cite30†Lun-Wei Ku , cite31†Andre Martins , cite32†Vivek Srikumar L73: Venue:\nL74:     cite33†ACL L75: SIG:\nL76: \nL77: Publisher:\nL78:     Association for Computational Linguistics\nL79: Note:\nL80: \nL81: Pages:\nL82:     279–299\nL83: Language:\nL84: \nL85: URL:\nL86:     cite34†https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.18/ L87: DOI:\nL88:     cite35†10.18653/v1/2024.acl-long.18†doi.org L89: Bibkey:\nL90: \nL91: Cite (ACL):\nL92:     Qingyun Wang, Doug Downey, Heng Ji, and Tom Hope. 2024. cite34†SciMON: Scientific Inspiration Machines Optimized for Novelty . In Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), pages 279–299, Bangkok, Thailand. Association for Computational Linguistics.\nL93: Cite (Informal):\nL94:     cite34†SciMON: Scientific Inspiration Machines Optimized for Novelty (Wang et al., ACL 2024)\nL95: Copy Citation:\nL96:     [Button: More options…]\nL97: PDF:\nL98:     cite23†https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.18.pdf L99: cite23†PDF cite0†Cite cite0†Fix data L100: \nL101: * * *\nL102: ##### Export citation\nL103: \nL104:     @inproceedings{wang-etal-2024-scimon,\nL105:         title = \"{S}ci{MON}: Scientific Inspiration Machines Optimized for Novelty\",\nL106:         author = \"Wang, Qingyun  and\nL107:           Downey, Doug  and\nL108:           Ji, Heng  and\nL109:           Hope, Tom\",\nL110:         editor = \"Ku, Lun-Wei  and\nL111:           Martins, Andre  and\nL112:           Srikumar, Vivek\",\nL113:         booktitle = \"Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)\",\nL114:         month = aug,\nL115:         year = \"2024\",\nL116:         address = \"Bangkok, Thailand\",\nL117:         publisher = \"Association for Computational Linguistics\",\nL118:         url = \"https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.18/\",\nL119:         doi = \"10.18653/v1/2024.acl-long.18\",\nL120:         pages = \"279--299\",\nL121:         abstract = \"We explore and enhance the ability of neural language models to generate novel scientific directions grounded in literature. Work on literature-based hypothesis generation has traditionally focused on binary link prediction{---}severely limiting the expressivity of hypotheses. This line of work also does not focus on optimizing novelty.\nL122: We take a dramatic departure with a novel setting in which models use as input background contexts (e.g., problems, experimental settings, goals), and output natural language ideas grounded in literature. We present SciMON, a modeling framework that uses retrieval of ``inspirations'' from past scientific papers, and explicitly optimizes for novelty by iteratively comparing to prior papers and updating idea suggestions until sufficient novelty is achieved.\nL123: Comprehensive evaluations reveal that GPT-4 tends to generate ideas with overall low technical depth and novelty, while our methods partially mitigate this issue. Our work represents a first step toward evaluating and developing language models that generate new ideas derived from the scientific literature. Code, data, and resources are publicly available for research purposes: \\url{https://github.com/eaglew/clbd}.\"\nL124:     }\nL125: Download as File\nL126: \nL127:     <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\nL128:     <modsCollection xmlns=\"http://www.loc.gov/mods/v3\">\nL129:     <mods ID=\"wang-etal-2024-scimon\">\nL130:         <titleInfo>\nL131:             <title>SciMON: Scientific Inspiration Machines Optimized for Novelty</title>\nL132:         </titleInfo>\nL133:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL134:             <namePart type=\"given\">Qingyun</namePart>\nL135:             <namePart type=\"family\">Wang</namePart>\nL136:             <role>\nL137:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL138:             </role>\nL139:         </name>\nL140:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL141:             <namePart type=\"given\">Doug</namePart>\nL142:             <namePart type=\"family\">Downey</namePart>\nL143:             <role>\nL144:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL145:             </role>\nL146:         </name>\nL147:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL148:             <namePart type=\"given\">Heng</namePart>\nL149:             <namePart type=\"family\">Ji</namePart>\nL150:             <role>\nL151:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL152:             </role>\nL153:         </name>\nL154:         <name type=\"personal\">\nL155:             <namePart type=\"given\">Tom</namePart>\nL156:             <namePart type=\"family\">Hope</namePart>\nL157:             <role>\nL158:                 <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">author</roleTerm>\nL159:             </role>\nL160:         </name>\nL161:         <originInfo>\nL162:             <dateIssued>2024-08</dateIssued>\nL163:         </originInfo>\nL164:         <typeOfResource>text</typeOfResource>\nL165:         <relatedItem type=\"host\">\nL166:             <titleInfo>\nL167:                 <title>Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)</title>\nL168:             </titleInfo>\nL169:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL170:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Lun-Wei</namePart>\nL171:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Ku</namePart>\nL172:                 <role>\nL173:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL174:                 </role>\nL175:             </name>\nL176:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL177:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Andre</namePart>\nL178:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Martins</namePart>\nL179:                 <role>\nL180:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL181:                 </role>\nL182:             </name>\nL183:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL184:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Vivek</namePart>\nL185:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Srikumar</namePart>\nL186:                 <role>\nL187:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL188:                 </role>\nL189:             </name>\nL190:             <originInfo>\nL191:                 <publisher>Association for Computational Linguistics</publisher>\nL192:                 <place>\nL193:                     <placeTerm type=\"text\">Bangkok, Thailand</placeTerm>\nL194:                 </place>\nL195:             </originInfo>\nL196:             <genre authority=\"marcgt\">conference publication</genre>\nL197:         </relatedItem>\nL198:         <abstract>We explore and enhance the ability of neural language models to generate novel scientific directions grounded in literature. Work on literature-based hypothesis generation has traditionally focused on binary link prediction—severely limiting the expressivity of hypotheses. This line of work also does not focus on optimizing novelty.\nL199: We take a dramatic departure with a novel setting in which models use as input background contexts (e.g., problems, experimental settings, goals), and output natural language ideas grounded in literature. We present SciMON, a modeling framework that uses retrieval of “inspirations” from past scientific papers, and explicitly optimizes for novelty by iteratively comparing to prior papers and updating idea suggestions until sufficient novelty is achieved.\nL200: Comprehensive evaluations reveal that GPT-4 tends to generate ideas with overall low technical depth and novelty, while our methods partially mitigate this issue. Our work represents a first step toward evaluating and developing language models that generate new ideas derived from the scientific literature. Code, data, and resources are publicly available for research purposes: https://github.com/eaglew/clbd.</abstract>\nL201:         <identifier type=\"citekey\">wang-etal-2024-scimon</identifier>\nL202:         <identifier type=\"doi\">10.18653/v1/2024.acl-long.18</identifier>\nL203:         <location>\nL204:             <url>https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.18/</url>\nL205:         </location>\nL206:         <part>\nL207:             <date>2024-08</date>\nL208:             <extent unit=\"page\">\nL209:                 <start>279</start>\nL210:                 <end>299</end>\nL211:             </extent>\nL212:         </part>\nL213:     </mods>\nL214:     </modsCollection>\nL215: Download as File\nL216: \nL217:     %0 Conference Proceedings\nL218:     %T SciMON: Scientific Inspiration Machines Optimized for Novelty\nL219:     %A Wang, Qingyun\nL220:     %A Downey, Doug\nL221:     %A Ji, Heng\nL222:     %A Hope, Tom\nL223:     %Y Ku, Lun-Wei\nL224:     %Y Martins, Andre\nL225:     %Y Srikumar, Vivek\nL226:     %S Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)\nL227:     %D 2024\nL228:     %8 August\nL229:     %I Association for Computational Linguistics\nL230:     %C Bangkok, Thailand\nL231:     %F wang-etal-2024-scimon\nL232:     %X We explore and enhance the ability of neural language models to generate novel scientific directions grounded in literature. Work on literature-based hypothesis generation has traditionally focused on binary link prediction—severely limiting the expressivity of hypotheses. This line of work also does not focus on optimizing novelty.\nL233: We take a dramatic departure with a novel setting in which models use as input background contexts (e.g., problems, experimental settings, goals), and output natural language ideas grounded in literature. We present SciMON, a modeling framework that uses retrieval of “inspirations” from past scientific papers, and explicitly optimizes for novelty by iteratively comparing to prior papers and updating idea suggestions until sufficient novelty is achieved.\nL234: Comprehensive evaluations reveal that GPT-4 tends to generate ideas with overall low technical depth and novelty, while our methods partially mitigate this issue. Our work represents a first step toward evaluating and developing language models that generate new ideas derived from the scientific literature. Code, data, and resources are publicly available for research purposes: https://github.com/eaglew/clbd.\nL235:     %R 10.18653/v1/2024.acl-long.18\nL236:     %U https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.18/\nL237:     %U https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.acl-long.18\nL238:     %P 279-299\nL239: Download as File\nL240: ##### Markdown (Informal)\n"
